<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Brabançonne</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1830">1830</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">La Brabançonne is the national anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, written during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgian-revolution">Belgian Revolution</a> of 1830 with words by Jenneval, a French actor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, and music by François Van Campenhout. Its name recalls Brabant, the old duchy at whose heart Brussels lies. The text has been revised several times, and the anthem is sung in French, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and German.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/la-brabanconne.jpg" alt="La Brabançonne" width="320" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Partition9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Brabant's name survives in the Belgian provinces of Flemish Brabant and Walloon Brabant and the Dutch province of North Brabant, the Brussels region lies in the centre of the old duchy, and the Belgian national anthem is called the <b>Brabançonne</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
